### What are the three delivery semantics?

The three semantics are a trade-off between the risk of loss and the risk of duplication, forced by the reality that networks and processes fail at the worst moments. At the National Bank of India, this trade-off directly affects account balances.

```text
Semantic       Offset Commit    Risk of Loss    Risk of Duplication
─────────────  ──────────────   ────────────    ───────────────────
At-most-once   Before process   Yes             No
At-least-once  After process    No              Yes
Exactly-once   Atomic with out  No              No
```

### How does at-most-once work?

The consumer commits its offset before processing. If it crashes mid-processing, the message is already marked done and is skipped on restart. Nothing is duplicated, but messages can be lost.

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import java.time.Duration;

KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
consumer.subscribe(List.of("bank-metrics"));

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    consumer.commitSync();  // Commit BEFORE processing

    for (ConsumerRecord<String, String> record : records) {
        // If we crash here, the message is already committed
        // and will be skipped on restart -- LOST
        metricsService.record(record.value());
    }
}
// Acceptable only for data you can afford to drop, like branch metrics
// NEVER acceptable for money movement
```

### How does at-least-once work?

The consumer commits the offset only after processing succeeds. If it crashes after processing but before committing, it reprocesses that message on restart.

```java
KafkaConsumer<String, Transaction> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
consumer.subscribe(List.of("bank-transactions"));

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Transaction> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Transaction> record : records) {
        Transaction txn = record.value();
        bankDatabase.applyDebit(txn);  // Process first
        // If we crash HERE, offset is not committed
        // On restart, the debit is applied AGAIN -- DUPLICATED
    }
    consumer.commitSync();  // Commit AFTER processing
}
// Default for most systems because losing data is worse than duplicating
// But duplicates are deadly in finance: applying a debit twice is wrong
```

### How do idempotent producers prevent duplicate writes?

Idempotent producers ensure that a producer retry does not write the same record twice into the log. The broker deduplicates using a producer ID and sequence number.

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import java.util.Properties;

Properties props = new Properties();
props.put("bootstrap.servers", "kafka-broker:9092");
props.put("key.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
props.put("value.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");

// Enable idempotent producer
props.put("enable.idempotence", "true");
// Implies acks=all, retries=Integer.MAX_VALUE, max.in.flight.requests.per.connection<=5

KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);

// Even if the producer retries due to a network glitch,
// the broker uses (producerId, sequenceNumber) to deduplicate
producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("bank-transfers", "TXN-1001", transferJson));
```

### How do Kafka transactions achieve exactly-once?

Kafka transactions commit produced messages and consumed offsets atomically in one transaction. This is the read-process-write exactly-once pattern.

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.OffsetAndMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.HashMap;

// Producer with transactional ID
Properties prodProps = new Properties();
prodProps.put("transactional.id", "bank-transfer-processor-1");
prodProps.put("enable.idempotence", "true");
KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(prodProps);
producer.initTransactions();

// Consumer with read-committed isolation
Properties consProps = new Properties();
consProps.put("isolation.level", "read_committed");
consProps.put("enable.auto.commit", "false");
KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(consProps);
consumer.subscribe(List.of("bank-transfers"));

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, String> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    producer.beginTransaction();
    try {
        for (var record : records) {
            // Process and produce output
            String enriched = enrichTransfer(record.value());
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("enriched-transfers", record.key(), enriched));
        }
        // Commit offsets AND produced messages atomically
        Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> offsets = getOffsetsToCommit(records);
        producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, consumer.groupMetadata());
        producer.commitTransaction();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        producer.abortTransaction();
    }
}
```

### How does Flink achieve exactly-once?

Flink uses checkpoint barriers and a two-phase commit sink. The barriers flow through the dataflow graph, and when all operators have checkpointed, the sink commits. On failure, Flink restores state from the last checkpoint and replays from that position.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.CheckpointingMode;

StreamExecutionEnvironment env = StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

// Enable exactly-once checkpointing
env.enableCheckpointing(60000);  // Every 60 seconds
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointingMode(CheckpointingMode.EXACTLY_ONCE);
env.getCheckpointConfig().setMinPauseBetweenCheckpoints(30000);

// Kafka source reads from committed checkpoint offset
// Kafka sink commits in two-phase commit aligned with checkpoints
// Result: exactly-once from source to sink
```

### What is effectively-once with idempotent writes?

When crossing into an external system that cannot participate in Kafka transactions, you often drop to at-least-once plus idempotent writes. This gives effectively-once results.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// At-least-once delivery + idempotent upsert = effectively-once
// The database uses the transaction_id as a unique key
// A duplicate message simply overwrites the same row

String upsertSql = """
    INSERT INTO bank_transfers (transaction_id, account_id, amount, status)
    VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)
    ON CONFLICT (transaction_id)
    DO UPDATE SET amount = EXCLUDED.amount, status = EXCLUDED.status
    """;

try (PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement(upsertSql)) {
    stmt.setString(1, txn.getTransactionId());  // Stable unique ID
    stmt.setString(2, txn.getAccountId());
    stmt.setBigDecimal(3, txn.getAmount());
    stmt.setString(4, txn.getStatus());
    stmt.executeUpdate();
}
// A replay after crash simply re-upserts: no duplicate effect
```

### When do you choose each semantic?

The choice is deliberate and depends on the data's value.

| Semantic | Use Case | Banking Example |
|---|---|---|
| At-most-once | Low-value, droppable data | Branch temperature metrics |
| At-least-once | Most data where loss is worse than duplication | Audit log events (deduplicate downstream) |
| Exactly-once | Money movement, balances | Debit/credit transactions |
| Effectively-once | External systems without transaction support | Writing transfers to PostgreSQL via upsert |

```java
// Decision tree in code
if (isMoneyMovement(txn)) {
    // Use Kafka transactions for exactly-once
    processWithTransaction(txn);
} else if (isAuditEvent(txn)) {
    // At-least-once with idempotent downstream
    processWithRetry(txn);
} else {
    // At-most-once for low-value metrics
    processFireAndForget(txn);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Exactly-once is not free: Kafka transactions add latency and complexity. Use them only where the data demands it.
- End-to-end exactly-once requires every component in the chain to participate. A non-transactional sink breaks the guarantee.
- The `read_committed` isolation level on the consumer ensures it never reads uncommitted (in-flight) transaction data.
- Flink's exactly-once and Kafka's exactly-once use different mechanisms but achieve the same goal.
- For most banking pipelines, the pragmatic pattern is at-least-once plus idempotent upserts, which gives effectively-once with simpler operations.
